rows.31:4a44:
With that, Iwashita turned her face away in a huff.
It didn't look like she would tell me anything more.
There was nothing for it. I'd go and hear the next person's story.{p}

rows.31:4cbc:
That painting is a self-portrait, painted by a student who used to go to this school.
Of course, she was in the art club.
Her name was Tomoko Shimizu.{p}

rows.31:4d44:
They say she was a very quiet person.
She loved painting, and she was aiming to get into art college.
But she was murdered while she was still painting that self-portrait, apparently.{p}

rows.31:5594:
......Did you hear something just now?
What was that sound?
..........Look!{p}

rows.31:5b18:
It was decided that it might be best to go ahead and burn it.
So they took it to the incinerator, but the painting was too big to fit inside.
There was nothing else for it, so they laid it on the ground and decided to set fire to it right there.{p}

rows.31:5fb4:
......After that, no one ever suggested burning that painting again.
Of course not. Nobody wants to die.
And that's why that portrait is still in the art room today.{p}

rows.31:6170:
......And that's the end of my story.
Now then, shall we go to the art room and put that painting back?
Who knows, we might hear something.{p}

rows.31:6208:
......Here we are.
Ah, the painting is still hanging right there on the wall.
{a}{f:|}, sorry, but would you take that painting down off the wall for me?{p}

rows.31:62a0:
Iwashita said that with a big smile on her face.
It felt like she was making fun of {f:me|me}, and it got under my skin.
So, scared as I was, I walked up to the painting without letting it show.{p}

rows.31:64fc:
......The eyes moved!
The eyes of the woman in the painting looked at {f:me|me}☆
Everything else was oil paint, but those eyeballs alone were solid and real, just like a living person's.{p}

rows.31:67c8:
Iwashita was still smiling.
"Well then, let's go back.
I wonder who will tell us the next story?"{p}
